438 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Dr. Pradip Dey \(Director, ICAR ATARI, Kolkata, Zone-V\)  Dr. Pradip Dey opened the session by drawing parallels with insights from the Cereal Systems Initiative for South Asia \(CSISA\) program, highlighting findings from comprehensive landscape diagnostic surveys conducted in Odisha and West Bengal. He emphasized that despite the availability of improved rice varieties, many farmers continue to grow older varieties, some over 25 to 30 years old, due to various constraints. While these traditional varieties may still perform reasonably well, Dr. Dey pointed out that newer options such as CR Dhan 810 and CR Dhan 811 offer significant advantages in yield and stress tolerance, and could serve as effective replacements for older varieties like Gayatri and Sarala.)
/E (Dr. Pradip Dey \(Director, ICAR ATARI, Kolkata, Zone-V\)  Dr. Pradip Dey opened the session by drawing parallels with insights from the Cereal Systems Initiative for South Asia \(CSISA\) program, highlighting findings from comprehensive landscape diagnostic surveys conducted in Odisha and West Bengal. He emphasized that despite the availability of improved rice varieties, many farmers continue to grow older varieties, some over 25 to 30 years old, due to various constraints. While these traditional varieties may still perform reasonably well, Dr. Dey pointed out that newer options such as CR Dhan 810 and CR Dhan 811 offer significant advantages in yield and stress tolerance, and could serve as effective replacements for older varieties like Gayatri and Sarala.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
439 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (He discussed that modern seeds are no longer just inputs for crop production but represent an integrated package, offering benefits such as disease resistance, stress tolerance, and nutritional enrichment, including biofortification. According to Dr. Dey, the cost of not adopting such improved varieties is disproportionately borne by marginalized and poor farming communities, as they miss out on gains in both productivity and nutrition. He advocated for rapid varietal replacement to ensure the timely transfer of these benefits.)
/E (He discussed that modern seeds are no longer just inputs for crop production but represent an integrated package, offering benefits such as disease resistance, stress tolerance, and nutritional enrichment, including biofortification. According to Dr. Dey, the cost of not adopting such improved varieties is disproportionately borne by marginalized and poor farming communities, as they miss out on gains in both productivity and nutrition. He advocated for rapid varietal replacement to ensure the timely transfer of these benefits.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Dr. Dey also urged the audience to view the seed sector through a value chain lens, rather than limiting it to the supply chain framework. While institutions like IRRI and CRRI have already established the upstream elements of the chain, such as inbound logistics and processing, attention now needs to focus on downstream components, including institutional development, data interoperability, marketing, and feedback mechanisms that can strengthen value delivery and support decision-making at all levels.)
/E (Dr. Dey also urged the audience to view the seed sector through a value chain lens, rather than limiting it to the supply chain framework. While institutions like IRRI and CRRI have already established the upstream elements of the chain, such as inbound logistics and processing, attention now needs to focus on downstream components, including institutional development, data interoperability, marketing, and feedback mechanisms that can strengthen value delivery and support decision-making at all levels.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
441 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (He proposed a shift toward landscape-based varietal targeting, advocating for the clustering of districts based on agroecological conditions such as soil fertility, water availability, and biotic and abiotic stress profiles. By categorizing varietal recommendations according to landscape profiles rather than administrative boundaries, Dr. Dey suggested that more tailored and effective seed packages could be developed.)
/E (He proposed a shift toward landscape-based varietal targeting, advocating for the clustering of districts based on agroecological conditions such as soil fertility, water availability, and biotic and abiotic stress profiles. By categorizing varietal recommendations according to landscape profiles rather than administrative boundaries, Dr. Dey suggested that more tailored and effective seed packages could be developed.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
